          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In a newborn with clubfoot, which of the four CAVE components is primarily respo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In a newborn with clubfoot, which of the four CAVE components is primarily responsible for preventing the foot from being placed flat on the ground?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cavus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cavus (the high arch from forefoot plantarflexion relative to the hindfoot) alters foot shape but is not the primary barrier to achieving a flat plantigrade position.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adductus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adductus (medial deviation of the forefoot and midfoot) changes the direction the foot points but does not prevent the sole from contacting the ground.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Varus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Varus (inward rotation of the calcaneum beneath the talus) tilts the foot onto its outer edge but is not the main reason the sole cannot lie flat.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Equinus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Equinus — fixed plantarflexion from a tight Achilles tendon and posterior capsule — locks the ankle downward, preventing dorsiflexion to a plantigrade position.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is equinus. All four CAVE components contribute to the deformity, but equinus (fixed plantarflexion from Achilles contracture) is the specific barrier to placing the sole flat on the ground."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Equinus locks the ankle in plantarflexion, making flat weight-bearing impossible. The name itself comes from the Latin for 'horse,' describing the hoof-like downward position."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
