          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A six-month-old infant is suspected of having developmental dysplasia of the hip</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A six-month-old infant is suspected of having developmental dysplasia of the hip. Which investigation is most appropriate at this age?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hip ultrasound using the Graf classification</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Graf ultrasound is the investigation of choice before four to six months, when the femoral head is entirely cartilaginous and invisible on X-ray. By six months, ossification has begun.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anteroposterior pelvic radiograph</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>After four to six months the femoral head ossification centre appears, making the AP pelvis X-ray the primary investigation. Hilgenreiner's and Perkin's lines can now assess femoral head position.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>MRI of the pelvis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MRI is reserved for pre-operative planning — specifically to detect soft-tissue blocks to closed reduction — and is not a first-line diagnostic investigation for DDH.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CT scan with three-dimensional reconstruction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CT carries significant ionising radiation and is not indicated as a first-line study for DDH at any age. An AP pelvis radiograph provides the necessary information without excess radiation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle is age-dependent imaging: ultrasound before four to six months (cartilaginous head invisible on X-ray), then AP pelvis radiograph once the ossification centre appears."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Once the femoral head begins to ossify at four to six months, plain radiography replaces ultrasound as the primary DDH investigation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
